9. Question 9/20 – Kubernetes Service & Networking Troubleshooting
Intermediate–Advanced
Scenario
You deployed your Flask application on EKS.
- 3 Pods are running.
- Deployment status shows 3/3 Available.
- Kubernetes Service is created successfully.
- AWS LoadBalancer DNS is generated.
- But when you open the DNS URL, you get:
      503 Service Temporarily Unavailable


Your Service YAML:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: proj3-service
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: proj3
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 5000


Your interview questions
1. Pods are running, but the application returns 503. What are the possible reasons?
A: in aws check : if security groups are blocking any traffic, check target ports are healthy or no, 
   in k8s check: check readiness probe, check end points ( kubectl get endpoints proj3-cluster -n web-apps), check svc )kubectl descrive svc proj3-svc, check if 
                application is listrning on port 5000, check if service selector matches the pod labels (kubectl get pods --show-labels -n web-apps)

2. How would you check whether your Service is correctly connected to your Pods? Give me the exact kubectl commands.
A: we will check the end points
    command: kubectl get endpoints proj3-svc -n web-apps
             then we can see the ip address of its selected pod

3. Suppose your Deployment has this label:
labels:
  app: flask-app


But your Service selector is:
selector:
  app: proj3


What happens? How would you fix it?

A: then dep and service wont be attached to each other, we needto edit the service file and change label to flask-app

4. Explain the traffic flow when a user accesses your LoadBalancer DNS. Start from the browser and explain how the request reaches your Flask container on port 5000.
A: user --> load balancer dns --> worker node --> k8s service --> flask pod: 5000 container port

5. Suppose your application works using kubectl port-forward, but doesn't work through the LoadBalancer. How would you troubleshoot the issue?
Bonus – Real interview follow-up
What's the difference between:
- Liveness Probe
- Readiness Probe
- Startup Probe
A: Liveness probe: checks if the application is alive. if it fails repetedly then restart container
   Readiness probe: checks if application is ready to recieve traffic. if it fails remove it from svc end points
   Startup Probe: It checks if the application has finished starting, protects the applications from liveness and readiness probes

